\section{Experimental design}
\label{sec:experiments}

\paragraph{Data and sampling.}
We use the nine datasets of the \texttt{ppi-python} package, as in \citet{angelopoulos2023ppi}, and a synthetic suite with known truth: \texttt{galaxies} \citep{willett2013galaxy}, \texttt{forest} \citep{bullock2020forest} with tree-cover predictions \citep{sexton2013global}, \texttt{alphafold} \citep{bludau2022structural} with predictions of \citet{jumper2021highly}, \texttt{gene\_expression} \citep{vaishnav2022evolution}, three \texttt{census} sets from ACS records \citep{ding2021retiring}, \texttt{plankton} \citep{olson2003automated,orenstein2015whoi} and \texttt{ballots} (the package's example data from a San Francisco election). The target is $\theta=\E[Y]$. On a real dataset the pool of labeled records is treated as the population, so the truth is its mean; each repetition draws $n$ labeled and $N$ unlabeled records independently with replacement, so records are i.i.d.\ and labels MCAR by construction. \texttt{ballots} has no outcome on its unlabeled split, so it supports width comparisons by resampling the labeled records only, not a coverage claim on the real split; \texttt{plankton} shifts between its two years, with the later mean known, so the i.i.d.\ assumption fails there on purpose. The synthetic suite covers an exactly affine $\E[Y\mid f]$ (no room, \cref{cor:noroom}), Gaussian designs with closed-form $\Delta$, sigmoid and non-monotone calibrations, binary $Y$ down to 1\% prevalence, and informative side information.

\paragraph{Arms.}
All arms see the same draws. With $f$ only (T1): the labels-only interval; PPI and PPI++ from \texttt{ppi-python}~0.2.3, the latter being the baseline, and the same PPI++ with $\hat\lambda$ unclipped (same formula, same draws; \cref{sec:setting}); PPI++ run on the calibrated score $\hat g(f)$; the cross-fitted estimator of \cref{sec:method}; the same estimator with $g$ chosen from \{PPI++, isotonic or spline calibration, gradient boosting\} by cross-validation inside each repetition, on folds separate from those used for the variance estimate; and the estimated floor $\hat V^\ast$ from a cross-fitted fit on the whole pool, which is not an interval but the variance that a cross-fitted estimator of that class would attain (an estimate within the class, not a bound). With $f$ and $X$ (T2, census tasks and AlphaFold): vector PPI++ and the OLS-adjusted mean (the strongest linear baseline), gradient-boosting AIPW, the calibrated estimator with $X$, the floor with $X$, and the selection among them. The headline arm, fixed before the runs, is the selection procedure of each tier; we call it \emph{the selected interval}.

\paragraph{Measures and decision rule.}
The headline measure is the paired width ratio $r=\text{width(arm)}/\text{width(PPI++)}$ on the same draws, with a 95\% interval over repetitions; PPI++ is the package's unless said otherwise. Since width scales as $n^{-1/2}$, $1-r^2$ is the fraction of labels saved. The rule was fixed before any run: a cell is \emph{no gain} if the interval for $r$ lies in $[0.98,1.02]$, a \emph{gain} if its upper bound is below $0.95$ and the arm is valid, and undecided otherwise. After the first results showed that the package's clip matters (\cref{sec:results}) we also read every ratio against the \emph{strongest valid baseline}: the narrower of the package's and the unclipped PPI++ among those whose coverage is valid in the cell (the narrower of the two if neither is), with the same margins. We report both classifications. An arm is valid in a cell if its coverage is not significantly below nominal (Wilson interval) and not below that of PPI++ beyond Monte Carlo error; an invalid arm's width is reported but not counted as a gain. Replacing the Wilson test by coverage $\ge0.94$ leaves the reference-$n$ classes and the range of $r$ unchanged, changes which baseline is used or whether it is valid in $2$ of the $9$ cells, without changing a class, and moves $5$ of the $113$ cells of \cref{fig:regime} (the only new gain is \texttt{gene\_expression} at $n=30$). Headline cells use at least 2000 repetitions and small-$n$ or rare-event cells at least 5000. Sample sizes are log-spaced in $\{30,\dots,10^4\}$ as each pool allows, with a reference $n$ fixed before the runs ($500$, except \texttt{forest}, whose pool allows $N=1000$ unlabeled records, where it is $200$); $N$ is the largest the pool allows under a stated cap, with $N/n\in\{1,4\}$ on three datasets.

Code, result files and the scripts that draw every figure and table are released with the paper; \cref{app:repro} gives the environment, data versions, seeds, compute and the script behind each exhibit.
